Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedbuiltin.shoddy — reckoner seed: the RUNTIME's own builtins
Rem
Rem THE ONE SEED THAT BRIDGES NO MACHINE. Every other seed in this
Rem directory takes a machine and converts its types at the bridge. This
Rem one takes the runtime: the words the engine dispatches directly,
Rem which no machine declares and which therefore no seed had ever
Rem carried to a prompt. There is nothing to convert — a builtin already
Rem speaks in numbers, strings, booleans and lists — so what this seed
Rem is made of is guards, and almost nothing else.
Rem
Rem WHICH BUILTINS, AND WHY THESE. The runtime dispatches 148 names. The
Rem core and the other seeds already register 58 of them, another 58 are
Rem deliberately unreachable, and the 32 registered here are what was
Rem left. That accounting is not a comment: tst/builtinsurfacetest.shoddy
Rem asserts the whole partition, so a builtin that is neither claimed nor
Rem excluded nor registered here fails the build. Read it for the rules
Rem that exclude the other 58 — a handle reaching the stack, a live
Rem device, the engine performing no effect, no cell to answer with, no
Rem guarded twin to build on, and a second spelling of a word the
Rem dictionary already has.
Rem
Rem THREE OF THOSE RULES BIT HERE and are worth naming at the door,
Rem because each looks like an oversight and is not:
Rem
Rem   * There is no PRINT. The engine performs no effect (R3.2) and
Rem     evaluation answers LINES for a shell to print, so a PRINT word
Rem     would write outside the transaction — the tape would not record
Rem     it, TAPESAVE would lose it, and the screen and the tape would
Rem     disagree.
Rem   * There is no DELETEFILE. READFILE, WRITEFILE and APPENDFILE all
Rem     abort too, and all three are here, rebuilt over TRYREADFILE and
Rem     TRYWRITEFILE the way seedfile.shoddy already rebuilt READLINES.
Rem     There is no TRYDELETEFILE, and FILEEXISTS narrows the window
Rem     without closing it.
Rem   * There is no SORT, no RND and no &. Each is already in the
Rem     dictionary under the name it chose — SORTL, RANDOM, and the core
Rem     + which joins two STRINGs. LEN is the near miss that is NOT one,
Rem     because LENGTH is list-only and refuses a STRING outright.
Rem
Rem THE GUARDS ARE THE WORK, and they divide in two. Some builtins ABORT
Rem — ASC on an empty string, NTH past the end, GAMMAP on a
Rem non-positive A — and those are the ordinary case R3.5(a) is about.
Rem The dangerous ones are the builtins that DO NOT abort and answer
Rem something wrong instead:
Rem
Rem   VAL is strtod. It answers 0 for anything it cannot read, so
Rem   "twelve" VAL is 0 and nothing says so. It is registered here
Rem   refusing unless ISNUMERIC agrees, which is the question VAL does
Rem   not ask. VALOR is the total form and is registered as it stands,
Rem   because a caller who supplied a fallback has said what they want.
Rem
Rem   WHERE THAT GUARD STOPS, and it stops deliberately: ISNUMERIC is
Rem   "would VAL succeed", not "is this wholly a number", so "3kg" VAL
Rem   passes it and answers 3. That laxity is strtod's and it is the
Rem   LANGUAGE's — reckoner's own tokenizer inherits it rather than
Rem   correcting it, so 3X on an entry line is 3 as well. A seed that
Rem   disagreed with the tokenizer about what counts as a number would
Rem   be a worse problem than the laxity is.
Rem
Rem   CHR casts to a 16-bit character, so 65536 CHR wraps silently to the
Rem   empty string rather than failing. Refused outside 0 .. 65535.
Rem
Rem   LEFT, RIGHT and MID clamp every argument rather than refusing, so a
Rem   count of 2.5 truncates to 2 and a start of -3 becomes 1. The
Rem   clamping is kept — a substring past the end is an ordinary answer —
Rem   but a FRACTIONAL count is refused, because that is a typo and not
Rem   an intention.
Rem
Rem ERROR AND ASSERT ARE INVERTED RATHER THAN DROPPED. Their whole
Rem purpose is to abort, which looks like an automatic exclusion; but
Rem turning an abort into a refusal is exactly what this layer is for, so
Rem "nope" ERROR refuses with "nope" and ASSERT refuses when its flag is
Rem false. What a program used to end the run with, a session now
Rem answers with.
Rem
Rem CALL IS THE ONE NEW CAPABILITY. Everything else here is a builtin
Rem wrapped. CALL applies a PROGRAM to a cell — ( x prog -- y ) — which
Rem was impossible before reckoner grew RckPureOver and RckProgOn, and
Rem which the combinators only ever offered over a whole list.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"

Rem ---- shared shapes ----------------------------------------------------

Rem A whole number that may be NEGATIVE, which RckWholeArg is not: its
Rem whole numbers start at zero, and an offset or a count can legitimately
Rem be below it. Refuses a fraction, which is the part that matters.
Def BiStepArg(word As String, c As Cell) As Result
    Select Case c
        Case CutNum(x)
            BiStepWhole(word, x)
        Case Else
            Err(RckKindWhy(word, "a NUMBER", c), 0)

Def BiStepWhole(word As String, x As Number) As Result
    If x <> Floor(x) Then
        Err(word & " needs a whole number, got " & Str(x), 0)
    Else
        Ok(x)

Rem One string in, one cell out — the shape half the text words have.
Def BiOnText(word As String, c As Cell, f As [ String -- Cell ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(s)
            RckVals({ Call(f, s) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem One number in, one number out — the shape the always-total
Rem arithmetic has.
Def BiOnNum(word As String, c As Cell, f As [ Number -- Number ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckNumArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(x)
            RckVals({ CutNum(Call(f, x)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem A string and a whole count, for LEFT and RIGHT.
Def BiTextCount(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ String Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg(word, Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(s)
            BiTextCountAt(word, s, Nth(vs, 2), f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BiTextCountAt(word As String, s As String, c As Cell, f As [ String Number -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case BiStepArg(word, c)
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ CutStr(Call(f, s, n)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Rem ---- text -------------------------------------------------------------

Def BiSeedText(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "LEN", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiOnText("LEN", First(vs), Fn(s) => CutNum(Len(s)))), "( s -- n )", "How many characters a string holds. LENGTH is the one for lists, and refuses a string.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "LEFT", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiTextCount("LEFT", vs, Left)), "( s n -- s2 )", "The first n characters, or the whole string if it is shorter. Refuses a fractional n.")
    Let s3 = RckReg(s2, "RIGHT", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiTextCount("RIGHT", vs, Right)), "( s n -- s2 )", "The last n characters, or the whole string if it is shorter. Refuses a fractional n.")
    Let s4 = RckReg(s3, "MID", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiMid(vs)), "( s start len -- s2 )", "len characters from position start, counting from 1. A range past the end is trimmed to what is there.")
    Let s5 = RckReg(s4, "INSTR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiInstr(vs)), "( s sub -- pos )", "Where sub first appears in s, counting from 1, or 0 when it does not. An empty sub answers 0.")
    Let s6 = RckReg(s5, "CHR", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiChr(vs)), "( n -- s )", "The character with code n. 0 answers the empty string. Refuses outside 0 to 65535, where the code would wrap silently.")
    Let s7 = RckReg(s6, "ASC", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiAsc(vs)), "( s -- n )", "The code of the first character. Refuses on the empty string, which has none.")
    Let s8 = RckReg(s7, "STR", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiStr(vs)), "( n -- s )", "A number written out as text, the way the stack shows it.")
    Let s9 = RckReg(s8, "VAL", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiVal(vs)), "( s -- n )", "Text read back as a number. Refuses anything that is not one, rather than answering 0 as the builtin would.")
    Let s10 = RckReg(s9, "VALOR", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiValor(vs)), "( s fallback -- n )", "The same, answering fallback for text that is not a number. Never refuses, because you said what to do instead.")
    RckReg(s10, "ISNUMERIC", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiOnText("ISNUMERIC", First(vs), Fn(s) => CutBool(IsNumeric(s)))), "( s -- flag )", "Whether VAL would read this text as a number — the question VAL itself does not ask.")

Rem MID clamps its start and its length rather than refusing, which is
Rem the right behaviour for a substring: asking for more than is there
Rem answers what is there. What is refused is a fractional start or
Rem length, since the builtin would truncate one silently.
Def BiMid(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("MID", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(s)
            BiMidNums(s, Nth(vs, 2), Nth(vs, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("MID did not answer")

Def BiMidNums(s As String, startC As Cell, lenC As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(BiStepArg("MID", startC), BiStepArg("MID", lenC))
        Case Pair(Ok(start), Ok(n))
            RckVals({ CutStr(Mid(s, start, n)) })
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(BiMidWhy(startC, lenC))

Def BiMidWhy(startC As Cell, lenC As Cell) As String
    Select Case BiStepArg("MID", startC)
        Case Err(w, at)
            w
        Case Else
            BiWhyOf("MID", BiStepArg("MID", lenC))

Def BiWhyOf(word As String, r As Result) As String
    Select Case r
        Case Err(w, at)
            w
        Case Else
            word & " did not answer"

Def BiInstr(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckTextArg("INSTR", Nth(vs, 1)), RckTextArg("INSTR", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(s), Ok(sub))
            RckVals({ CutNum(Instr(s, sub)) })
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("INSTR needs two STRINGs")

Rem The builtin casts to a 16-bit character, so a code above 65535 wraps
Rem round and answers some other character, or the empty string. That is
Rem a wrong answer and not an abort, which is exactly the kind this layer
Rem exists to catch.
Def BiChr(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckWholeArg("CHR", First(vs))
        Case Ok(n)
            BiChrAt(n)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("CHR did not answer")

Def BiChrAt(n As Number) As RckWork
    If n > 65535 Then
        RckWorkBad("CHR: NEEDS A CODE FROM 0 TO 65535, AND " & Str(n) & " WOULD WRAP")
    Else
        RckVals({ CutStr(Chr(n)) })

Def BiAsc(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("ASC", First(vs))
        Case Ok(s)
            BiAscAt(s)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("ASC did not answer")

Def BiAscAt(s As String) As RckWork
    If s = "" Then
        RckWorkBad("ASC: THE EMPTY STRING HAS NO FIRST CHARACTER")
    Else
        RckVals({ CutNum(Asc(s)) })

Def BiStr(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckNumArg("STR", First(vs))
        Case Ok(x)
            RckVals({ CutStr(Str(x)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("STR did not answer")

Rem The most dangerous builtin in the runtime, at a prompt. Val is
Rem strtod: it takes a leading number off a longer token and answers 0
Rem for anything it cannot read at all, so "twelve" VAL is 0 and "3kg"
Rem VAL is 3, and neither says so. IsNumeric is the question it does not
Rem ask, so it is asked here first.
Def BiVal(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("VAL", First(vs))
        Case Ok(s)
            BiValAt(s)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("VAL did not answer")

Def BiValAt(s As String) As RckWork
    If Not IsNumeric(s) Then
        RckWorkBad("VAL: " & RckQuoteMark() & s & RckQuoteMark() & " IS NOT A NUMBER — VALOR TAKES A FALLBACK FOR THIS")
    Else
        RckVals({ CutNum(Val(s)) })

Rem No guard, deliberately: a caller who supplied a fallback has already
Rem said what an unreadable string should answer, and refusing would
Rem override them.
Def BiValor(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckTextArg("VALOR", Nth(vs, 1)), RckNumArg("VALOR", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(s), Ok(d))
            RckVals({ CutNum(ValOr(s, d)) })
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("VALOR needs a STRING and a NUMBER")

Rem ---- numbers ----------------------------------------------------------

Def BiSeedNums(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "NEGATE", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiOnNum("NEGATE", First(vs), Negate)), "( x -- -x )", "The number with its sign turned round.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "ATN", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiOnNum("ATN", First(vs), Atn)), "( x -- rad )", "The arctangent, in radians. ATN2 is the one that knows which quadrant.")
    Let s3 = RckReg(s2, "ATN2", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiAtn2(vs)), "( y x -- rad )", "The angle of the point (x, y), in radians, in the right quadrant.")
    Let s4 = RckReg(s3, "TANH", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiOnNum("TANH", First(vs), Tanh)), "( x -- y )", "The hyperbolic tangent. Saturates towards 1 and -1, so it never overflows.")
    Let s5 = RckReg(s4, "ERF", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiOnNum("ERF", First(vs), Erf)), "( x -- y )", "The error function: odd, and between -1 and 1.")
    Let s6 = RckReg(s5, "GAMMAP", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiGammap(vs)), "( a x -- p )", "The regularized lower incomplete gamma. Refuses unless a is positive and x is not negative.")
    RckReg(s6, "BETAI", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiBetai(vs)), "( a b x -- p )", "The regularized incomplete beta. Refuses unless a and b are positive and x is between 0 and 1.")

Def BiAtn2(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckNumArg("ATN2", Nth(vs, 1)), RckNumArg("ATN2", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(y), Ok(x))
            RckVals({ CutNum(Atn2(y, x)) })
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("ATN2 needs two NUMBERs")

Rem Both of these Error on exactly these conditions in the runtime. The
Rem same tests, asked first.
Def BiGammap(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckNumArg("GAMMAP", Nth(vs, 1)), RckNumArg("GAMMAP", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(a), Ok(x))
            BiGammapAt(a, x)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("GAMMAP needs two NUMBERs")

Def BiGammapAt(a As Number, x As Number) As RckWork
    If a <= 0 Then
        RckWorkBad("GAMMAP: A MUST BE POSITIVE")
    Else
        If x < 0 Then
            RckWorkBad("GAMMAP: X MUST NOT BE NEGATIVE")
        Else
            RckVals({ CutNum(GammaP(a, x)) })

Def BiBetai(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckNumArg("BETAI", Nth(vs, 1)), Pair(RckNumArg("BETAI", Nth(vs, 2)), RckNumArg("BETAI", Nth(vs, 3))))
        Case Pair(Ok(a), Pair(Ok(b), Ok(x)))
            BiBetaiAt(a, b, x)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("BETAI needs three NUMBERs")

Def BiBetaiAt(a As Number, b As Number, x As Number) As RckWork
    If a <= 0 Or b <= 0 Then
        RckWorkBad("BETAI: A AND B MUST BOTH BE POSITIVE")
    Else
        If x < 0 Or x > 1 Then
            RckWorkBad("BETAI: X MUST BE BETWEEN 0 AND 1")
        Else
            RckVals({ CutNum(BetaI(a, b, x)) })

Rem ---- lists ------------------------------------------------------------

Def BiSeedLists(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "NTH", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiNth(vs)), "( list k -- v )", "The k-th element, counting from 1. Refuses an index the list does not have.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "SETNTH", 3, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiSetnth(vs)), "( list k v -- list2 )", "The list with its k-th element replaced. The original is unchanged. Refuses an index the list does not have.")
    Let s3 = RckReg(s2, "ISEMPTY", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiIsempty(vs)), "( list -- flag )", "Whether the list holds nothing.")
    Let s4 = RckReg(s3, "PREPEND", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiPrepend(vs)), "( v list -- list2 )", "The list with v put on the front. APPEND's opposite end.")
    RckReg(s4, "DIM", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiDim(vs)), "( n init -- list )", "A list of n copies of init. Refuses a negative or fractional n.")

Rem NTH and SETNTH both Error on an index outside 1..Length. Asked here
Rem first, and the message names the range that WAS available, because
Rem "out of range" without it is a message you have to go and count for.
Def BiNth(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckListArg("NTH", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(xs)
            BiNthAt("NTH", xs, Nth(vs, 2), Fn(l, k) => Nth(l, k))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("NTH did not answer")

Def BiNthAt(word As String, xs As List Of Cell, kc As Cell, f As [ List Of Cell Number -- Cell ]) As RckWork
    Select Case RckWholeArg(word, kc)
        Case Ok(k)
            BiNthIn(word, xs, k, f)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " did not answer")

Def BiNthIn(word As String, xs As List Of Cell, k As Number, f As [ List Of Cell Number -- Cell ]) As RckWork
    If k < 1 Or k > Length(xs) Then
        RckWorkBad(word & ": THERE IS NO ELEMENT " & Str(k) & " — THE LIST HOLDS " & Str(Length(xs)))
    Else
        RckVals({ Call(f, xs, k) })

Def BiSetnth(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckListArg("SETNTH", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(xs)
            BiSetnthAt(xs, Nth(vs, 2), Nth(vs, 3))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("SETNTH did not answer")

Def BiSetnthAt(xs As List Of Cell, kc As Cell, v As Cell) As RckWork
    BiNthAt("SETNTH", xs, kc, Fn(l, k) => CutList(SetNth(l, k, v)))

Def BiIsempty(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckListArg("ISEMPTY", First(vs))
        Case Ok(xs)
            RckVals({ CutBool(IsEmpty(xs)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("ISEMPTY did not answer")

Def BiPrepend(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckListArg("PREPEND", Nth(vs, 2))
        Case Ok(xs)
            RckVals({ CutList(Prepend(Nth(vs, 1), xs)) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("PREPEND did not answer")

Rem The builtin builds an ARRAY, and there is no ARRAY cell at the
Rem keyboard (R4.16), so this builds the LIST that means the same thing.
Rem The negative size the builtin Errors on is refused first either way.
Def BiDim(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckWholeArg("DIM", Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Ok(n)
            RckVals({ CutList(Map(Range(1, n), Fn(k) => Nth(vs, 2))) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("DIM did not answer")

Rem ---- whole files ------------------------------------------------------

Rem All three builtins abort on a path they cannot use, so none of them
Rem is called. READFILE goes through TRYREADFILE and WRITEFILE through
Rem TRYWRITEFILE; APPENDFILE has no guarded twin at all, so it reads
Rem what is there and writes it back with the new text on the end — the
Rem same honest emulation seedfile.shoddy's APPENDLINE already makes,
Rem and not the real O_APPEND.
Rem
Rem These are the WHOLE-FILE pair to seedfile's line-oriented one:
Rem READLINES splits, READFILE does not.

Def BiSeedFiles(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "READFILE", 1, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => BiReadfile(vs)), "( path -- s )", "The whole file as one string, newlines and all. READLINES is the one that splits it.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "WRITEFILE", 2, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => BiWritefile(vs)), "( path s -- ok )", "Write the string to path, replacing what was there; True on success.")
    RckReg(s2, "APPENDFILE", 2, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => BiAppendfile(vs)), "( path s -- ok )", "Add the string to the end of path, making it if it is not there; True on success.")

Def BiReadfile(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("READFILE", First(vs))
        Case Ok(path)
            BiReadAt(TryReadFile(path))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("READFILE did not answer")

Def BiReadAt(r As Result) As RckWork
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(text)
            RckVals({ CutStr(text) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("READFILE did not answer")

Def BiWritefile(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    BiWroteWith("WRITEFILE", vs, Fn(old, s) => s)

Def BiAppendfile(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    BiWroteWith("APPENDFILE", vs, Fn(old, s) => old & s)

Def BiWroteWith(word As String, vs As List Of Cell, f As [ String String -- String ]) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckTextArg(word, Nth(vs, 1)), RckTextArg(word, Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(path), Ok(s))
            RckVals({ CutBool(TryWriteFile(path, Call(f, BiExisting(word, path), s))) })
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad(word & " needs a path and a STRING")

Rem What is already at the path, or nothing when there is no file or it
Rem cannot be read. WRITEFILE never asks for this — its own combiner
Rem drops it — so only APPENDFILE pays for the read.
Def BiExisting(word As String, path As String) As String
    If word = "WRITEFILE" Then
        ""
    Else
        BiTextOr(TryReadFile(path))

Def BiTextOr(r As Result) As String
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(text)
            text
        Case Else
            ""

Rem ---- the session it is running in -------------------------------------

Def BiSeedSession(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "ARGS", 0, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => RckVals({ CutList(Map(Args(), CutStr)) })), "( -- list )", "The arguments the mill itself was started with, as a list of strings.")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "CLOCK", 0, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => RckVals({ CutList(Map(ToList(Clock()), CutNum)) })), "( -- list )", "The wall clock as seven numbers: year, month, day, hour, minute, second, millisecond. STAMP is the readable one.")
    RckReg(s2, "SLEEP", 1, RckTouch(Fn(vs) => BiSleep(vs)), "( ms -- )", "Wait that many milliseconds and answer nothing. A count of 0 or less waits not at all.")

Def BiSleep(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckNumArg("SLEEP", First(vs))
        Case Ok(ms)
            BiSleptFor(ms)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("SLEEP did not answer")

Def BiSleptFor(ms As Number) As RckWork
    Sleep(ms)
    RckVals({ })

Rem ---- CALL, and the two words that used to end the run -----------------

Def BiSeedCall(st As RckState) As RckState
    RckReg(st, "CALL", 2, RckPureOver(Fn(sst, vs) => BiCall(sst, vs)), "( x prog -- y )", "Run the program on x and answer what it left. MAP does this over a whole list; this is the one element.")

Rem The only word here that is not a builtin wrapped. RckProgOn runs the
Rem program against the WHOLE session — a program may name a word
Rem defined a line earlier and may RCL a register — and answers the one
Rem cell it left, or the engine's own reason why it could not.
Def BiCall(st As RckState, vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckProgArg("CALL", Nth(vs, 2))
        Case Ok(ts)
            BiCallOn(st, ts, Nth(vs, 1))
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("CALL did not answer")

Def BiCallOn(st As RckState, ts As List Of String, x As Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckProgOn("CALL", st, ts, x)
        Case Ok(v)
            RckVals({ v })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("CALL did not answer")

Rem Inverted rather than dropped. Both exist to end the run, and turning
Rem that into a refusal is the whole of what this layer does — so a
Rem program that used ERROR to stop now gives a session a reason, and
Rem the next line still evaluates.
Def BiSeedStop(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "ERROR", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiError(vs)), "( s -- )", "Refuse the line, giving s as the reason. In a program this ends the run; here it answers.")
    RckReg(s1, "ASSERT", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => BiAssert(vs)), "( flag s -- )", "Refuse the line with reason s when flag is false, and do nothing at all when it is true.")

Def BiError(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckTextArg("ERROR", First(vs))
        Case Ok(s)
            RckWorkBad(s)
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("ERROR did not answer")

Def BiAssert(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckBoolArg("ASSERT", Nth(vs, 1)), RckTextArg("ASSERT", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(p), Ok(s))
            BiAssertThat(p, s)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("ASSERT needs a BOOLEAN and a STRING")

Def BiAssertThat(p As Boolean, s As String) As RckWork
    If p Then
        RckVals({ })
    Else
        RckWorkBad(s)

Rem ---- the seed -----------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedBuiltin(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s0 = RckSeeding(st, "builtin")
    Let s1 = BiSeedText(s0)
    Let s2 = BiSeedNums(s1)
    Let s3 = BiSeedLists(s2)
    Let s4 = BiSeedFiles(s3)
    Let s5 = BiSeedSession(s4)
    Let s6 = BiSeedCall(s5)
    BiSeedStop(s6)
